# Pulse tick: digest (~/explore) — the multi-source morning digest
# Runs the /daily-digest skill directly (not /pulse tick).
# Inspect runs: journalctl --user -u pulse-digest.service -n 20
#
# LOOP ID = digest (renamed 2026-07-27, explore-8tit — was `daily-digest`
# 2026-06-24..2026-07-27). The fleet convention is `unit stem == ledger row`
# (pulse-dive/dive, pulse-desk/desk); the digest was the last outlier, and Zig
# wants it to read `digest` in the harness loop list. `--loop %p` below derives
# the loop id from THIS unit's name, so the rename carries automatically to
# harness-manifest.json's `timer` field and to pulse-bounces.jsonl.
#
# The ledger row moved with it (`daily-digest` -> `digest`, declared in
# ~/explore/refs/pulse.md); the 33 historical rows keep the old name and are
# archived, never rewritten. Renaming a unit leaves exactly ONE stale file
# behind — systemd's own ~/.local/share/systemd/timers/stamp-<unit>, deleted in
# the same change so last_fire does not point at the predecessor's run.
#
# NOT renamed: the skill is still invoked as /daily-digest and still lives at
# ~/explore/.claude/skills/daily-digest/; the steering file is still
# refs/daily-digest.md; the tmux window was already `digest`.
#
# WINDOW NAME = digest (changed 2026-07-24, by Zig's request — was `explore`
# 2026-06-28..2026-07-24). The digest fires into its OWN `digest` window
# instead of mingling with the explore agent's session. (The SESSION it lives
# in is ExecStart's --session flag, below — `zig-computer` since 2026-07-31,
# `work` before that. Don't restate it up here; this comment said `work:digest`
# for two days after the move — explore-6abm.)
#
# WHY THE MOVE: the 2026-07-24 run went partial — 5 of 7 fetch subagents
# produced no output for ~45 min and were stopped (bead explore-pvio). The
# session had ALSO run three back-to-back vibe-explore /pulse ticks, and the
# first read was concurrency contention. Zig's correction: they did not stall
# on contention — they timed out / got FORGOTTEN, and the same session later
# recovered and finished them. The shared session got DISTRACTED. A dedicated
# window removes the interleaving entirely: the digest's fetch fan-out is the
# only thing that session is doing.
#
# WHAT'S GIVEN UP: the deliberate context-mingling with the explore agent
# (the 2026-06-28 rationale — the digest feeds exploration). That handoff is
# now via artifacts, not shared context: the Vibes card the digest queues is
# what the explore agent crawls, and refs/session-handoff--digest.md +
# digests/YYYY-MM-DD.md + refs/digest-ledger.jsonl carry the rest. Both were
# already absolute-path anchored, so nothing had to move.
#
# REQUIREMENT that makes this work (do NOT remove): /daily-digest is a REAL
# skill in the explore tree at ~/explore/.claude/skills/daily-digest/ (migrated
# 2026-07-07 from weekly-reporting, where it used to live symlinked). Claude
# Code's skill discovery walks .claude/skills/ from the session cwd, so with
# --dir ~/explore it resolves natively. (The earlier 2026-06-26 "use wr-digest,
# NOT a window Zig opens" lesson was about an ACCIDENTAL collision that no-op'd
# because the skill wasn't resolvable there; living natively in ~/explore
# removes that failure mode.) The skill anchors all state to absolute paths
# ($EX=~/explore), so it works regardless of cwd.
#
# pulse-inject creates the window if it is missing (new-window -d, cwd --dir)
# and cold-starts claude in it, so no manual setup is required after a reboot.
[Unit]
Description=Pulse tick: digest (tmux-injection into its own `digest` window — no explore-session interleaving)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# Anchor --dir at ~/explore so the session (cold-start or warm) is
# explore-anchored and the native /daily-digest skill resolves.
ExecStart=%h/.agents/agents/scheduler/pulse-inject.sh --loop %p --fresh --dir %h/explore --session zig-computer --window digest --cmd "/daily-digest" --launch %h/explore/tools/tick-jail/tick-jailed.sh --launch-detect bwrap
